[{"expectations":"Check whether each lesson teaches one idea: one skill or up to three closely linked skills that a learner would naturally learn together. Skills must be generic actions worded in the form that language's course pages use (the verb first in English and the Romance languages, verb-final in German, Turkish, Japanese and Korean), with a canonical topic, a one-sentence idea, a concrete example and a real-life use. When the input lesson was too big and the output has several lessons, the split must follow natural boundaries (for example measures of center apart from measures of spread), keep teaching order, and give each lesson a canonical title, a scoped description and a concrete can-do line. Notice a lesson that bundles unrelated ideas, a split that separates ideas that define each other, and a skill that is a topic name instead of an action. A lesson with no single idea, or two separate ones, leaves the learner without one clear thing to take away; one weak skill or boundary is a smaller cost.","id":"oneIdea","label":"One idea"},{"expectations":"Check the screen plan a writer will follow. At every level, the first screen is a scene that asks nothing: an everyday moment where the idea shows up, ending on the question the lesson answers, never \"In this lesson…\". Right after the first explanation comes one guess that doesn't count, a prediction that neither contains its answer nor repeats the screen before's calculation. Explanations must go from something concrete to the name to symbols, one idea per screen. Checks must make the learner use the idea (predict, calculate, classify, spot the error) with a named tempting wrong answer, not repeat a sentence. Hard skills need a worked example followed by a similar problem with less help. The last screen applies the skills to a realistic situation for this audience. At every level, the first explanation comes before the first check. Briefs must be concrete enough for a writer: each states its idea in a plain sentence with the case that shows it (numbers, cases, examples). Notice weak checks, hooks or applications, checks that mostly ask for recall, a worked example without its follow-up, and briefs too vague to write from: the writer will produce a lesson that is unclear or doesn't make the learner think.","id":"screenPlan","label":"Screen plan"},{"expectations":"Check activities and visuals. An activity belongs only where doing beats reading (a value that changes with another, an order to rebuild, a guess to test), must use a template that fits the interaction, and must be tied to what the screen teaches; no activity is fine when none helps. Every screen a learner would otherwise have to picture in their head to follow or to answer, questions included, shows it (how something looks, is built, is arranged or moves, a place, a curve, data to compare, a thing nobody sees in daily life), and every other screen stays text only, the opening scene included: a visual is never there to fill a screen. The kind follows the app's order: data as a table, a bar or line chart or a timeline in the brief with exact values (visual null), something to work with as an activity whose template draws it, and a picture in `visual` only for what those can't show; a picture names every part, place or label the screen needs. Go through every guess, check, activity and application on its own: one whose answer depends on seeing something (where a part or a place is, a numbered label, a setup's positions or distances, two looks compared) and shows nothing is a missing visual, even when an earlier screen showed it. Notice as much a screen that leaves the learner to imagine something as a decorative visual (a scene or a person that shows nothing to see, a table that restates its sentence, the same picture again with nothing new), and a picture where a table, chart or timeline fits. A question the learner can't answer as planned because it doesn't show what it asks about, a lesson about something visual with no visual, and visuals or activities that are mostly decoration cost the learner most; one missing, misplaced or decorative visual, or one poorly chosen activity, is a smaller cost.","id":"activitiesAndVisuals","label":"Activities & visuals"},{"expectations":"Check fit to the level, the scope and the language. Overview: plain words and stories, no formulas, equations, code or notation, light checks. Beginner: everyday words a 12-year-old can follow, a formula only when the idea needs it and after the intuition. Intermediate and advanced: precise terms and notation after the intuition. The plan must stay inside this lesson: ideas from the other lessons in the chapter get at most a sentence of context, an idea an earlier lesson taught is not explained again, and the plan never reuses a case, set of numbers or question the chapter's other lessons list. No filler screens (why the topic matters in general, history, study tips, summaries). All text must be in the requested language variant (US English, Brazilian Portuguese, Mexican Spanish). Formulas or notation in an overview lose the reader it's for; one level or scope slip, or one reused case, is a smaller cost, and several, or the wrong language variant, a larger one.","id":"levelAndScope","label":"Level, scope & language"}]